WEBVTT

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:04.043
Five hundred years ago, a single
book could cost more than a house.

00:00:04.314 --> 00:00:04.864
Why?

00:00:05.174 --> 00:00:07.284
Because every page was copied by hand.

00:00:07.575 --> 00:00:09.905
A monk could spend
months on a single Bible.

00:00:10.216 --> 00:00:14.426
Then, around 1450,
a goldsmith in Germany had an idea.

00:00:14.802 --> 00:00:16.812
His name was Johannes Gutenberg.

00:00:17.137 --> 00:00:20.687
He made little metal letters that
could be moved around and reused.

00:00:21.168 --> 00:00:25.098
He added oil-based ink,
and a press like the ones used for wine.

00:00:25.449 --> 00:00:28.859
Around 1455, his Bible came off the press.

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:30.849
Printing itself was not new.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:34.160
China and Korea had movable
type centuries earlier.

00:00:34.471 --> 00:00:37.441
But Gutenberg's press spread
across Europe like wildfire.

00:00:38.032 --> 00:00:38.842
By 1500,

00:00:39.232 --> 00:00:41.382
Europe had printed millions of books.

00:00:41.712 --> 00:00:44.302
Books got cheaper,
and more people learned to read.

00:00:44.623 --> 00:00:46.913
Ideas could now travel faster than armies.

00:00:47.324 --> 00:00:52.114
In 1517, Martin Luther's arguments
were printed and spread in weeks.

00:00:52.509 --> 00:00:54.819
Science, news, and new ideas followed.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.650
One small invention changed how
the whole world shares knowledge.

